.TH GPS-TOF 5 "2026-11-05" "gps-tof 0.3.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
gps-tof.yaml \- configuration file format for gps-tof
.SH DESCRIPTION
The configuration is a YAML mapping.  All keys are optional except \fBsites\fR.
Numeric values written with an exponent should carry a sign (\fB64.0e+6\fR) because
YAML 1.1 parsers read \fB64.0e6\fR as a string; gps-tof coerces such strings, but the
signed form is unambiguous.
.SS Top-level settings
.TP
.B project
Report title.
.TP
.B clock_hz
Tick clock frequency in Hz (default 64e6).
.TP
.B legacy_unit_s
A second time unit to express the flight time in (default 500e-9 s).
.TP
.B earth
Mapping with \fBmodel\fR (wgs84 | grs80 | sphere) and \fBsphere_radius_m\fR.  The model not
chosen is the reference row of the correction ledger.
.TP
.B offset_normal
geodetic | radial: the "up" direction used when applying antenna-to-endpoint offsets.
\fBradial\fR is the plane tangent to a sphere (p/|p|), \fBgeodetic\fR the ellipsoid normal.
.TP
.B apply_sagnac
true to add the Sagnac term to the total offset (it is always reported).
.TP
.B rounding
round | floor | ceil for the integer tick count.
.TP
.B precision_digits
Decimal places in the report.
.TP
.B physics
\fBneutrino_mass_ev\fR, \fBneutrino_energy_gev\fR: if both are given the mass-induced delay
t m^2 / 2E^2 is reported.
.TP
.B trigger_window
\fBbefore_us\fR, \fBafter_us\fR, \fBmicroslice_us\fR: the window [t - before, t + after) in ticks
and the number of readout slices it can overlap.
.TP
.B sensitivity
\fBenabled\fR, \fBarcsec_step\fR, \fBheight_step_m\fR, \fBoffset_step_m\fR: steps used for the
numerical sensitivities.
.SS sites
A list of mappings.  Each site needs \fBname\fR and either \fBlat\fR/\fBlon\fR or \fBfscs_xyz_ft\fR.
.TP
.B name, id
Display name and short identifier (default: slug of the name).  Baselines refer to the id.
.TP
.B lat, lon
Decimal degrees or strings such as "41 50 23 N", "41:50:23N", "48 22.715780 N", "88 16 13 W".
Longitude is east-positive when numeric.
.TP
.B height_m, datum, geoid_separation_m
Height and what it is: \fBellipsoid\fR (height above the WGS84 ellipsoid, what a GPS receiver
reports in $POLYP or $GPGGA altitude + geoid separation) or \fBmsl\fR (orthometric altitude,
the $GPGGA altitude field); with \fBmsl\fR the geoid separation N is required and
h = H + N is used.  The conversion appears as a ledger row.
.TP
.B fscs_xyz_ft
Fermilab site coordinates (DUSAF/FSCS) in US survey feet: x east, y north, z elevation above
the DUSAF datum.  Converted with the double stereographic projection of Beams-doc-1148
(origin A0, azimuth 38 16 48.01429, F0 = 1.000029251309483, dH_NAVD88 = -0.17308 m,
N = -32.78456 m).  Overrides lat/lon/height.
.TP
.B offset_enu_m, offset_note
Vector from the surveyed point to the physics endpoint in the local East, North, Up frame,
metres.  A detector 120 m below the antenna is [0, 0, -120].
.TP
.B cable_length_m, cable_velocity_factor, cable_delay_ns
Antenna cable delay (reported as a calibration item, never added to the flight time).
.TP
.B uncertainty
Mapping of \fBlat_arcsec\fR, \fBlon_arcsec\fR, \fBheight_m\fR (1-sigma values),
\fBoffset_m\fR (worst case along the chord) and \fBquantization_arcsec\fR (coordinate
resolution; gives an RMS and a worst-case entry).  Used by the error budget.
.TP
.B source, notes
Free text carried into the report.
.SS beam_axis
\fB{from, to, label}\fR: the reference axis is the line from site \fBfrom\fR through site \fBto\fR.
When present, the report contains a "Beam axis" section (azimuth and elevation at the origin, and
for every site the off-axis angle at the origin, the distance along the axis, the transverse
distance with its right/up components and a clock angle), every baseline reports the opening
angle between its chord and the axis with right/up components and the off-axis position of both
endpoints, and every opening angle reports the angle of each chord to the axis.  Command line:
\fB\-x FROM:TO\fR, \fB\-\-no\-axis\fR.
.SS baselines
List of \fB{from, to, label, axis_to}\fR.  \fBaxis_to\fR names a site such that the angle between
from\(->to and from\(->axis_to is reported as the off-axis angle.  If the list is absent every
pair of sites is computed.
.SS opening_angles
List of \fB{vertex, a, b, label, axis_to}\fR.  The angle at \fBvertex\fR between the chords
vertex\(->a and vertex\(->b is reported with its horizontal (azimuth) and vertical (elevation)
components at the vertex, the transverse distance of each endpoint from the other chord, the
difference of the two flight times in ticks, and, with \fBaxis_to\fR, the off-axis angle of
each chord and whether the two endpoints lie on the same side of the axis.
.SS timing_shifts
List of fixed offsets added to the flight time.  Each has \fBname\fR and either
\fBseconds\fR, or \fBturns\fR, \fBbuckets_per_turn\fR and \fBrf_hz\fR (value = turns *
buckets / f_RF).  \fBrf_uncertainty_hz\fR adds an error-budget entry.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
project: "NOvA NuMI -> Ash River timing offsets"
clock_hz: 64.0e+6
earth: {model: wgs84}
trigger_window: {before_us: 20, after_us: 30, microslice_us: 50}
beam_axis: {from: target, to: minos-fd, label: "NuMI beam axis"}
sites:
  - name: "NuMI target (TDR 1998 design)"
    id: target
    fscs_xyz_ft: [100327.838, 97780.188, 626.743]
  - name: "NOvA Near Detector"
    id: nd
    lat: "41 50 23 N"
    lon: "88 16 13 W"
    height_m: 228.0
    offset_enu_m: [0, 0, -120]
    uncertainty: {quantization_arcsec: 1.0, height_m: 33.0, offset_m: 10.0}
  - name: "Ash River Ant-2"
    id: fd2
    lat: "48 22.715780 N"
    lon: "92 49.876930 W"
    height_m: 348.638
    datum: ellipsoid
    offset_enu_m: [0, 0, -19.8]
baselines:
  - {from: nd, to: fd2, label: "ND -> FD"}
  - {from: target, to: fd2, axis_to: decay-end}
opening_angles:
  - {vertex: target, a: nd, b: fd2, label: "ND vs FD from the target", axis_to: decay-end}
timing_shifts:
  - {name: "MI 141-turn shift", turns: 141, buckets_per_turn: 588, rf_hz: 53.1034e+6, rf_uncertainty_hz: 1000}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gps-tof (1)
